# Murtaugh RUNTIME NODE credentials — keep this file secret.
#
# This is ~/.config/murtaugh/node/.env. Every secret lives here; the config
# file beside it references these variables as ${VAR} and never holds the
# values, so the `murtaugh troubleshoot` bundle (which collects the .yaml
# files) cannot leak them. A value exported in the real environment overrides
# the one here.
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line. No quotes needed; no spaces around '='.
#
# There are deliberately no SLACK_* variables here. A node has no Slack
# connection: it reaches Slack only through the gateway it dials, and the
# workspace's app and bot tokens stay on the gateway. Naming them in this file
# would be an invitation to fill them in, on the one machine #170 is explicit
# must never hold them.
#
# This node's own credential is not here either. It is a FILE — `node-token`
# beside this one — so the sandboxed model this node runs cannot read it out of
# the environment. Mint one on the gateway with `murtaugh node token mint`.

# --- LLM providers (only the ones your native agents use) ---
# The variable NAME is what an agent profile's `api_key_env:` points at.
GEMINI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OpenAI/Anthropic-compatible third parties reuse the families above with a
# base_url override (set per-agent with `murtaugh cfg agent …`). Give each its
# own key var:
# ZAI_API_KEY=
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
# KIMI_API_KEY=

# --- External MCP servers (optional; referenced from mcp_servers[*].env) ---
# VAULTRE_TOKEN=
